# Python
**/.venv/
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Environment / secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Audio test artifacts
*.wav

# ONNX models — run brain/tools/fetch_models.py instead
brain/models/

# Who Humalien has met. Local to each machine, never committed.
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# --- 3D, and the one rule here that is a licence and not a preference -----
#
# The head mesh is royalty-free under a No AI licence: free to use in the
# build, not free to redistribute, and never to be fed to a generative 3D
# tool. It stays out of git entirely — research archive 01/cad/head_ref.py
# reproduces the import and scaling from wherever you keep the file locally.
**/female_human_head.*

# Anyone else's models, kept locally as a measuring reference and not ours
# to redistribute. Extension-based on purpose, so it holds wherever they land.
*.3mf
*.step
*.obj
*.mtl
*.fbx

# Blender rolling backups and the v1 file. The v2 working .blend is now
# TRACKED. The rule that blanket-ignored every .blend was written when
# HEAD_FACE and HEAD_CRANIUM - cut from the No-AI-licence sculpt - lived
# inside those files. That geometry went in 55f802d and no head mesh file
# remains anywhere in the repo, so the licence argument no longer covers the
# v2 file.
#
# Still true, and the reason to keep blend commits deliberate rather than
# habitual: it is binary that git cannot diff, and every save writes a whole
# new copy into history. research archive 01/mockups/eye_lids.py rebuilds
# every part from five numbers and an arc, so the blend is a convenience,
# not the source of truth.
# If it starts bloating the repo, move it to git-lfs.
*.blend[0-9]
Eye_Study_v1.blend

# Exports are build output and are NOT tracked in v2. v1 tracked them, on the
# reasoning that having the exact file that was printed is useful - and it
# was, twice. What it also did was leave superseded STLs in the working tree
# next to current ones, so which file to print became a question you had to
# answer from git rather than from the folder. Two of them were printed from
# after being superseded.
#
# The deciding argument is the licence, not the clutter: the head shells are
# cut straight from the No-AI sculpt, exporting them is fine and committing
# them is redistribution. Ignoring the whole folder removes the chance of
# getting that wrong by hand.
#
# research archive 01/cad/rack.py and .../cad/eye_v2.py rebuild every part,
# and .../cad/checks.py gates them before any of it reaches a bed.
exports/
print/
*.stl

# ...with one deliberate exception. "test prints/" is the current set to put
# on a bed, tracked so it can be pulled on the other laptop and so there is a
# record of exactly what was printed when something comes out wrong.
#
# The licence argument above does not reach it: every part in there is built
# from primitives by cad/eye_rig.py, and the two eyeballs are generated
# spheres. No geometry cut from the No-AI head sculpt goes in this folder,
# and nothing that is may ever be added to it.
!test prints/
!test prints/*.stl
!eye rig/
!eye rig/*.stl

# Editors / OS
.vscode/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Blender working files. The .blend is the source of truth for geometry but it
# is a 6 MB binary that changes wholesale on every save, so it lives on the
# machine, not in the history. Back it up yourself.
*.blend
*.blend1

# Printed output. STLs are generated from cad/ - regenerate them, do not
# commit them. A stale folder of STLs beside a current one has already cost
# two parts printed from a superseded file.
exports/
eye rig/

# STL is build output, same rule as exports/ - the script is the source
stl/
